{
 "lead": "Castigliano's theorem turns a strain energy formula into a deflection. Write the strain energy U of a linear elastic body as a function of an applied load P, differentiate with respect to P, and the result is the displacement Δ at that load, along that load. Some syllabi cover it under energy methods.",
 "prereq": "You need <a href=\"#strain-energy-in-axial-bending-and-torsion-loading\">2.1</a> for U = P²L/(2AE), U = ∫M²/(2EI) dx and U = T²L/(2GJ).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Why the slope of U is a deflection",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a linear elastic body, ∂U/∂P = Δ at the load P, along P; ∂U/∂M = θ. This is Castigliano's second theorem."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "∂U/∂P = Δ        ∂U/∂M = θ",
     "where": "U is strain energy (J); P is load (N); Δ is displacement (m) along P; M is moment (N·m); θ is rotation (rad)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Load and deflection grow together, so U = ½PΔ. For an axial member, U = P²L/(2AE), so dU/dP = PL/(AE) = Δ."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "An aluminium strut has L = 1.2 m, A = 250 mm², E = 70 GPa. With P in kN, U = 0.03429 P² J. At P = 20 kN, dU/dP = 0.06857 P = 1.371 J/kN, which is 1.371 mm. Directly, Δ = PL/(AE) = 1.371 × 10⁻³ m."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Read backwards, ∂U/∂Δ = P, Castigliano's first theorem."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "The dummy load where no load acts",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The theorem needs a load where you want the deflection. Usually there is none, so invent one."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Apply a fictitious load Q at that point, along the wanted deflection. For rotation, use a fictitious moment Q.",
      "Write U with Q kept as a symbol.",
      "Differentiate ∂U/∂Q; the result contains Q.",
      "Set Q = 0 only after differentiating."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Set Q = 0 inside U first, and ∂U/∂Q = 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A reaction acts at a support; ∂U/∂R gives the support's own movement, zero on a rigid support. Used the other way round, ∂U/∂R = 0 is the compatibility equation that finds a redundant reaction R. For deflections, differentiate real loads or a dummy load at a free point."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
